> mostly to the aforesaid computer-phone interface,
> instead of being for multi-purpose everday use.

Speech-recognition for lojban has been talked of before, but I 
don't think its value lies in being understandable in loud 
environments. Lojban sticks to five easily differentiated vowels, 
but so does Spanish. Lojban's virtue is its unambiguous and 
easily-parsed grammar, which should make life easier for 
whoever will eventually pioneer the technology.

I don't consider this to be the main goal of lojban, though. Voice 
recognition is being made for natural languages already, even if 
it requires relatively more work than lojban would require. 
Lojban's main goal is to improve thought by making it easier to 
express ideas.

